# tests/QUARANTINE — tests EXCLUDED from `make test` (the green baseline), each
# with a reason. Read by tools/run-scm-tests.sh. Format: one repo-relative path
# per line; everything after `#` is the reason. Blank lines ignored.
#
# Three kinds of exclusion, all explicit so nothing rots silently:
#   needs-*   — hermetic only with an external daemon/lib/service running.
#   bench-*   — a benchmark or genuinely long solve, not a pass/fail unit test.
#   broken:*  — REAL red, self-contained, TRACKED debt to fix (the work-list).
#
# Run the quarantined set for visibility (without gating) with:
#   RUN_QUARANTINED=1 make test-all
# Fix a `broken:` entry, delete its line, and it rejoins the baseline.
#
# ── AUDITED 2026-08-14: every line below was RUN, not re-read ────────────────
# A reason written once is a claim about the world that stops being checked, and
# "quarantined" ages into "forgotten" — so each entry was executed and its stated
# reason compared against what actually happened. Two were wrong:
#
#   test-mesh-smoke.scm was listed as hanging to timeout without a Reticulum
#   mesh. It does not hang. It prints `SKIP: needs rns-bridge debug binary +
#   node + rns-wasm pkg` and exits 0 in 10 s. A test that declines cleanly is
#   the opposite of what this file is for — it belongs in the baseline, where
#   its skip is visible and where it will actually run the day the deps exist.
#
#   test-planner-service.scm was the sole `broken:` entry, filed for a record
#   identity clash. It passes — 65 checks, 6 s. The clash is gone.
#
# Both rejoined the baseline. The remaining twelve were confirmed: the ten
# needs-* entries all fail fast against absent daemons (6–22 s, exit 1), and the
# two bench entries are excluded by runtime, not by breakage.
#
# Re-run this audit the same way — execute the list, do not read it.

